{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III inherited the county at a young age after his father was assassinated. Because he was still a child his mother acted as regent until he came of age. His early years were marked by political instability and the constant threat of Muslim forces in the region. The County of Tripoli was one of the four major Crusader states and held strategic importance along the eastern Mediterranean coast. Raymond III spent much of his youth learning the ways of governance and warfare under the guidance of experienced nobles and knights.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nAs an adult Raymond III became a key figure in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He served as regent of Jerusalem during the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was chosen for this role because of his noble lineage and his experience in governance. His regency was controversial as he often clashed with other nobles and military orders such as the Knights Templar. He favored diplomacy over aggressive warfare which put him at odds with more militant factions. Despite this he managed to maintain relative peace with neighboring Muslim rulers for a time. His efforts to negotiate truces were seen as pragmatic by some and as weak by others. His leadership style reflected the complex realities of ruling a fragile Christian state surrounded by powerful Muslim neighbors.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a central role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was present at the battle and survived the defeat but was captured by Saladin. After his release he returned to Tripoli where he died later that year. His death marked the end of an era of cautious diplomacy 